# Security overrides for <workspace-name>

> Workspace-specific extension of `~/.kiro/steering/pre-commit-security.md`.
> List identifiers that are PII / personal / internal-only for THIS project —
> agent will block on commit if these strings appear in tracked files outside
> whitelisted contexts.
>
> ⚠️ This file (the REAL one — `security-overrides.md` without `.example`)
> MUST be gitignored. Tracking it would expose exactly the strings we want
> to block. The `.example` template here is safe to commit.
>
> Setup:
>   cp .kiro/steering/security-overrides.md.example \
>      .kiro/steering/security-overrides.md
>   # Then edit the real file with your project's actual identifiers.

## Sensitive identifiers (block on commit)

### Owner — personal info
- Telegram handle: `@<your-telegram-username>`
- Telegram user_id: `<your-numeric-user-id>`
- Personal email: `<your-email>@<provider>.com`
- Real machine username path: `/Users/<your-name>/`, `C:\Users\<your-name>\`
- Real bot username (production): `<your-real-bot-username>`

### Internal infra
- Internal Git host: `<git.your-company.com>`
- Internal org path: `<your-org>/`
- Private API endpoints: `https://<internal>.your-company.com`
- Production database hosts: `<prod-db>.<region>.rds.amazonaws.com`

### Customer / client confidential (if applicable)
- Customer codenames: `<ProjectAcme>`, `<CodenameDelta>`
- Customer-specific feature flags / IDs
- Vendor license keys for proprietary deps

### Generic placeholders allowed (NOT blocked)
- `<you>`, `<user>`, `<your-handle>`
- `your_telecode_bot`, `placeholder_*`, `example_*`
- Numeric placeholders like `123456789` in `*.example.*` files
- Test scrubber fake patterns matching shape but synthetic: `1234567890:ABCdef...`

## Workspace-specific .gitignore additions
Beyond the universal list in the global rule, this workspace's `.gitignore`
should also cover:
- `<your specific runtime/cache dirs>`
- `<your specific config files that hold secrets>`

## Whitelist contexts (allowed match locations)

Files that MAY contain the sensitive patterns above without triggering a block:
- `tests/scrub*.test.ts` (or equivalent) — verify the scrubber redacts
  known-shape patterns; the SHAPE must match, LITERAL is always synthetic
- Markdown code-fence blocks documenting "DO NOT commit X" with explicit
  warning text upstream

Anything else with a hit → BLOCK.

## Owner override log

Last updated: <YYYY-MM-DD> by <maintainer>. Update when:
- Owner changes Telegram handle / email / personal accounts
- New internal hostnames / infra references appear in code / docs
- New "fake but real-shaped" test fixtures get added
- New customer engagements bring NDA-bound identifiers

## Verification

```bash
# Workspace-specific scan script (if present)
./scripts/security-scan.sh
# Or via the publish pipeline:
./scripts/publish-to-github.sh --dry-run
# Should report: "Security scan passed."
# If any sensitive identifier slipped back into tracked files, scan FAILS.
```
